/* V64 — arrivals become events instead of drizzle.

   The other half of the same play feedback: too many monsters, and the game is boring because you
   just press them. V63 answered the second half by giving a kill an area. This answers the first,
   and it is worth being precise about what "too many" actually meant, because simply spawning fewer
   was not it — that was tried first and it made the arena empty 87% of the time, which is not calm,
   it is nothing.

   Hostiles have always arrived one at a time, at a random x, on a timer. Six of those over ten
   seconds is six separate interruptions, each demanding one tap and offering no relationship to the
   other five. That is what makes a crowd feel like a pile of chores: not the number, the *lack of
   structure*. Six unrelated things to do is worse than one thing with six parts, even though the
   count is identical.

   So arrivals come in formations. A row, a wedge, a keystone, a column — three to five hostiles
   entering together, all the same size, spaced against V63's blast reach so the first link always
   connects and the second never quite does, falling at a shared speed so the shape survives the
   descent.

   What that changes:

   - **One event, not four chores.** A wedge is a single thing to read and a single plan to make. The
     screen can hold five hostiles and still ask the player for one decision, which is the opposite
     of the arrangement that produced the complaint.
   - **The shape is the puzzle.** V63's blast shrinks 22% per link, so a formation is never simply
     "tap the middle and win". Break a row at the centre and both wings go, but their own blasts fall
     short of each other and of the tail: three of four, never four. A keystone has one hostile that
     takes the whole formation and three that take only one. Where you break the shape decides how
     much of it goes, and each shape asks a different question.
   - **It gives the game a rhythm.** Formation, resolve, breathe, formation. The old drizzle had one
     dynamic level from start to finish; alternating pressure and quiet is most of what makes an
     arcade game feel composed rather than merely busy.
   - **It reads better.** Four dots in a recognisable shape is easier for the eye to parse than four
     dots at random, at the same count. Part of "the game doesn't look good" is visual noise, and
     structure is the cheapest possible fix for noise.

   Held to a ceiling on purpose: a formation is only sent into a screen that is nearly clear. A pack
   arriving on top of a pack is the pile of chores again, with extra steps. */

const V64={
 /* Chance an arrival is a formation rather than a single, ramping with the wave. Never 1: a lone
    straggler between formations is what keeps the shapes feeling like events rather than the metre
    of the game. */
 chance:(wave)=>Math.min(.78,.34+wave*.055),
 /* Formation size, likewise ramping. Five is the ceiling because a sixth member does not add a
    decision -- it is past the point where the blast falloff can plausibly reach, so it is just one
    more tap. */
 size:(wave)=>Math.min(5,3+Math.floor((wave-1)/4)),
 /* Spacing between members, derived from V63's blast reach rather than written as a number, so the
    two can never drift apart. Members sit at 92% of the distance a first blast can reach: the first
    link always connects, and the second -- 22% shorter -- never quite does. That is the decision the
    formation is asking about. Hard-coding this as a constant is how the first version went wrong;
    when the blast radius changed, the spacing did not, and the shapes stopped meaning anything. */
 gap:(r)=>(V63.radius*r+r*.35)*.92,
 /* Never send a formation while this many hostiles already exist. This is the actual answer to "too
    many monsters": density is capped by refusing to stack events, not by starving the arena.

    Counted across the whole field rather than only what is on screen, and that distinction turned
    out to matter more than the number. Hostiles enter from above the top edge, so a formation is
    invisible for its first second or so; gating on the visible count meant a formation still on its
    way down read as an empty arena and brought another one, and another. Measured, that left a
    median of seven hostiles queued off-screen with nothing at all in front of the player -- the
    arena felt empty and then arrived all at once. */
 busy:2,
 /* Where the formation's lowest member starts, just above the top edge. Everything else is hung off
    this, so however deep a shape is, its first hostile crosses into view immediately and the rest
    follow. The first version hung the shape off the anchor's own spawn y instead, which put a deep
    formation's tail five hundred pixels above the screen and left the player looking at an empty
    arena for five seconds while it queued up out of sight. */
 entry:-60,

 /* ---- The shapes ----

    Both axes are in units of `gap`, and `gap` is 92% of how far a first blast reaches. That one fact
    is what makes these puzzles rather than decoration, so it is worth writing the three distances
    down: a first blast reaches about 1.09 gaps, the second link about 0.87, the third about 0.70.
    Every offset below is chosen against those numbers.

      row       three abreast one gap apart, plus a tail out of reach behind them. Tap the middle and
                both wings go -- but their own blasts fall 13% short of each other and of the tail, so
                it is three of four, never four. The straightforward shape, and the one that teaches
                the rule.
      wedge     an apex with two wings set back. The wings are 0.99 gaps from the apex -- inside
                reach -- and 1.76 from each other. Only the apex breaks it. The first version put
                them at 1.07 gaps, which is inside reach on paper and, measured, missed: the margin
                was thinner than the jitter in where a hostile actually ends up. A shape whose
                intended answer works four times in five is a shape that teaches the wrong lesson.
      keystone  four corners around one hostile sitting low of centre. The centre is within 0.83 gaps
                of all four, so it takes the whole formation; a corner reaches the centre and nothing
                else. One right answer worth five kills, three wrong ones worth two. This is the
                shape the mechanic exists for.
      column    single file, a gap apart. Each blast takes the hostile above and below and stops, so
                a column is cut into threes wherever you break it -- the shape that rewards patience,
                because it arrives over a couple of seconds rather than all at once.

    Deliberately all no more than two gaps wide. A portrait phone is 390 points across and a hostile
    is up to 78 across; the first version had shapes four gaps wide, which on a real screen meant the
    spacing was squeezed to fit and four hostiles arrived as one unreadable blob. Depth is free on a
    portrait screen, width is not. */
 shapes:[
  {id:'row',     at:[[-1,0],[0,0],[1,0],[0,-1.15],[0,-2.15]]},
  {id:'wedge',   at:[[0,0],[-.88,-.45],[.88,-.45],[0,-1.15],[0,-2.15]]},
  {id:'keystone',at:[[0,-.575],[-.6,0],[.6,0],[-.6,-1.15],[.6,-1.15]]},
  {id:'column',  at:[[0,0],[0,-1],[0,-2],[0,-3]]}
 ]
};

/* Members are spawned through the captured base rather than through `spawnOrb`, so every layer below
   this one -- V21's wards and divers, V9's naming, V14's combat stats, the art variants -- applies
   to them exactly as it would to any other hostile, while this layer does not re-enter itself and
   have each member bring a formation of its own. The flag is belt and braces for any future layer
   that ends up calling back in. */
let v64Building=false;

function v64Live(){
 return game.running
  &&!game.paused
  /* Duels replay one server-authoritative spawn list. Adding client-side members would put two
     players in different arenas, which is the one thing the duel design cannot survive. */
  &&game.mode!=='duel'
  &&!game.boss
  &&!(game.v35Hush>0);
}

const v64SpawnBase=spawnOrb;
spawnOrb=function(type){
 v64SpawnBase(type);
 if(v64Building||!v64Live())return;
 const anchor=game.orbs.at(-1);
 /* Rivals are a scripted duel event and titans are not in this list at all; neither should ever
    arrive as one of five. */
 if(!anchor||anchor.type==='rival')return;

 if(game.orbs.length>V64.busy)return;
 if(rng()>=V64.chance(game.wave))return;

 const shape=V64.shapes[Math.floor(rng()*V64.shapes.length)];
 let size=Math.min(V64.size(game.wave),shape.at.length);

 /* How wide the shape is, in gap units. Needed before the gap is fixed, because a formation that
    does not fit has to lose the gap rather than lose the spacing -- and squeezing the spacing is
    what turns four hostiles into one unreadable blob. */
 const bounds=(n)=>{
  let lo=Infinity,hi=-Infinity;
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){lo=Math.min(lo,shape.at[i][0]);hi=Math.max(hi,shape.at[i][0]);}
  return {lo,hi,mid:(lo+hi)/2};
 };
 const extent=(n)=>{const b=bounds(n);return b.hi-b.lo;};

 /* The members are spawned before anything is measured, because the two things that decide the
    layout -- how far apart they must sit and how far the blast reaches -- both depend on their
    radii, and the base spawner rolls a different radius for every hostile.

    Then they are all set to the largest of those radii. That is a deliberate loss of variety and it
    fixes two faults at once. Visually, a 78-across hostile next to a 50-across one at a spacing
    computed from the smaller of the two overlaps, which is exactly the blob the first screenshot
    showed. Mechanically, blast reach scales with radius, so a mixed formation had members whose
    blasts could not reach the neighbour that could reach them -- the shape said one thing and the
    chain did another. A formation reads as a squad now, and every member's blast reaches exactly as
    far as every other's. */
 const members=[anchor];
 v64Building=true;
 try{
  for(let i=1;i<size;i++){
   v64SpawnBase(undefined);
   const born=game.orbs.at(-1);
   if(born&&born!==members[members.length-1])members.push(born);
  }
 }finally{v64Building=false;}
 size=members.length;
 let r=0;
 for(const m of members)r=Math.max(r,m.r);
 for(const m of members)m.r=r;

 const want=V64.gap(r)*(isMobile?1:1.15);
 const room=Math.max(120,W-2*(r+16));
 /* Members are dropped rather than squeezed when a shape will not fit. Spacing IS the mechanic: four
    hostiles with the shape intact is a better arrival than five crammed to the width of a phone. The
    dropped ones are removed from the field, not stacked on top of the survivors. */
 while(size>2&&extent(size)*want>room){
  const cut=members.pop();
  const at=game.orbs.indexOf(cut);
  if(at>=0)game.orbs.splice(at,1);
  size--;
 }
 const gap=extent(size)>0?Math.min(want,room/extent(size)):want;

 /* Centred on the shape's own midpoint, not on its first slot: several of the shapes are not
    symmetric about slot zero, and centring on the wrong point is how a formation ends up with its
    last member pressed against the edge. */
 const margin=r+12+extent(size)*gap/2;
 const cx=W/2-bounds(size).mid*gap+(rng()-.5)*Math.max(0,W-margin*2);
 let lowest=-Infinity;
 for(let i=0;i<size;i++)lowest=Math.max(lowest,shape.at[i][1]);
 const cy=V64.entry-lowest*gap;

 for(let i=0;i<size;i++){
  const [dx,dy]=shape.at[i];
  const member=members[i];
  member.x=Math.max(r+8,Math.min(W-r-8,cx+dx*gap));
  member.y=cy+dy*gap;
  /* A shared speed is what makes it a formation rather than five things that happened to start
     together. V21 can still pull one member out as a diver, and that is left alone deliberately --
     a shape that loses a member on the way down is a better read than a shape that cannot. */
  member.speed=anchor.speed;
  member.v64Pack=shape.id;
 }
};
